      .cc-vitals-stress
        .cc-banner Stress
        //- Hand-expanded from +fillLeft (see the SCSS comment on
        //- .cc-stress-track below for how its "selected/unselected" fill
        //- coloring works) so a second, hidden radio group - one radio per
        //- box, sharing the same 1-20 values - can be interleaved as
        //- siblings of the visible ones. That hidden group's value is kept
        //- equal to stress_max by reusing calcStressMax as its own
        //- calculation, so whichever one of its radios is "checked" marks
        //- the max-value boundary. Pure CSS can't compare two numbers, but
        //- it CAN compare checked states via sibling selectors - the same
        //- trick fillLeft itself already uses for the selected/unselected
        //- split - so putting each box's own hidden radio right after it
        //- lets `:checked ~ .fill-left__radio` grey out (and, via
        //- pointer-events, disable) every visible box past the boundary.
        //-
        //- The visible track itself uses +radio, not +checkbox (which is
        //- +fillLeft's own default widget for the non-noClear case this
        //- was expanded from) - confirmed live and reproduced locally that
        //- checkboxes here let a double-click net out to "nothing in the
        //- whole group is checked" (checking then immediately unchecking
        //- the same box), and with NOTHING checked, the CSS below has no
        //- :checked anchor to peel any box's coloring off the shared
        //- default - which is "selected"/filled, not "unselected"/empty -
        //- so every box renders filled. A real radio group can't reach
        //- that state at all: clicking an already-checked radio is a
        //- native no-op, and clicking a different one always atomically
        //- (un)checks exactly one radio in the group, so some box stays
        //- checked from the first click onward. The trade-off is losing
        //- "click the current top box again to clear by 1" - clicking any
        //- lower box directly to jump straight to that value is a fair
        //- replacement, and is also how stress_max_marker above already
        //- works. The value=0 radio restores the "nothing taken yet"
        //- all-empty look this replaces losing to that same double-click
        //- bug - hidden the same way stress_max_marker is (see its own
        //- display:none rule below), not a literal +hidden() input, since
        //- a genuine type="hidden" input can never match :checked at all
        //- (confirmed the hard way: that was the ORIGINAL value=0 marker
        //- here, and it never once griyed out anything, live or locally).
        .cc-stress-track
          +radio({name: 'stress', value: 0, checked: ''}).fill-left__radio.cc-fill-left-zero
          each value in Array.from({length: 20}, (_, i) => i + 1)
            +radio({name: 'stress', value}).fill-left__radio
            +radio({
              name: 'stress max marker',
              value,
              trigger: {calculation: 'calcStressMax'}
